BANK.push(
{"id":"p1","d":5,"q":"A bank ships its mobile app every two weeks. Each release waits on a two-week manual penetration test, so features slip by a full cycle. The banking regulator expects at least one independent test per year. The AppSec team is one engineer, the backend is Kotlin on Kubernetes, and the product owner has asked security to stop being the bottleneck. Most releases are UI tweaks, but roughly one in five touches login, payments, or a new partner integration. What should the architect propose?","o":["Threat model at design, gate builds on SAST and SCA, and send only releases touching auth, payments, or trust boundaries to manual testing, plus the yearly test","Replace the manual test with a DAST scan that blocks on high findings in the release pipeline, and contract the yearly independent test to satisfy the regulator","Keep the manual test but run it in parallel with the next sprint, shipping first and folding the findings into the following release cycle","Deploy RASP on the Kotlin backend so releases can ship without pre-release testing while runtime protection blocks exploitation of whatever slips through"],"a":[0],"e":"Risk-based routing keeps delivery moving: automation covers common code flaws, threat modeling catches design flaws, and the scarce manual effort goes to the one in five releases that change sensitive paths. DAST alone misses design and logic flaws. Shipping before testing means sensitive changes reach production untested. RASP is a compensating runtime control, not a substitute for testing."},
{"id":"p2","d":5,"q":"A retailer's Java monorepo has about 400 third-party dependencies. Legal wants license risk tracked and security wants known CVEs flagged. During discovery the architect finds that roughly a third of the libraries are vendored JAR files copied into lib/ folders years ago, often renamed, with no entry in any pom.xml. The team already runs a SAST tool tuned for Spring. Which capability matters MOST when selecting the new tool?","o":["Software composition analysis that identifies components by binary fingerprint or hash, not only by parsing build manifests","Software composition analysis with reachability analysis, so findings in dependencies the code doesn't call are deprioritized","SBOM generation from the Maven build at compile time, published as CycloneDX for each release of the monorepo","SAST with taint tracking that follows data from Spring controllers into library calls and flags unsafe sinks"],"a":[0],"e":"The deciding fact is the vendored, renamed JARs with no manifest entries. Only fingerprint or hash matching will find them. Reachability analysis is useful for triage but works on what the tool already found. A Maven-generated SBOM inherits the same manifest blind spot. SAST looks at first-party code flows and doesn't identify component versions or licenses."},
{"id":"p3","d":5,"q":"A medical device software vendor started shipping SBOMs after hospital customers demanded them. Now hospital security teams feed the SBOMs into scanners and open dozens of tickets per release for CVEs in bundled libraries. Engineering has confirmed most of those flagged functions are not compiled in or not reachable. Support is overwhelmed and two large customers threaten to pause deployments. The SBOMs are already signed and in CycloneDX format. What should the vendor add?","o":["VEX statements published with each release stating, per CVE, whether the product is affected and why","Richer SBOM fields, such as component hashes, supplier names, and dependency relationships","SLSA provenance attestations for each release showing which builder produced the artifacts and from which source commit","A public security advisory page listing each bundled library with its upgrade schedule and support end dates"],"a":[0],"e":"The problem is not identifying components but communicating exploitability. VEX is built for that: it lets the vendor say 'not affected, vulnerable code not present' per CVE so scanners can suppress noise. Richer SBOM fields improve matching but still produce the same CVE hits. Provenance proves build integrity, not exploitability. An advisory page helps humans but doesn't feed the customers' scanners."},
{"id":"p4","d":5,"q":"An attacker phished a developer and pushed a change to the .gitlab-ci.yml file on a new feature branch. The pipeline ran on that branch, pulled the production deploy token from CI variables, and pushed a backdoored image to production. The company has since enforced phishing-resistant MFA. The main branch already requires two approvals, and all commits must be signed through the company's keyless signing flow tied to SSO. Which control MOST directly closes the path used?","o":["Rotate all CI variables and move secrets into an external vault that pipelines query at runtime using their own short-lived job token","Bind production deploy credentials to protected branches and approval-gated environments so unreviewed branches can't get them","Add a policy scanner that checks CI configuration files for suspicious commands and fails the pipeline when it finds them","Require signed commits on feature branches as well as on main, verified against each developer's hardware-backed signing key"],"a":[1],"e":"The attacker's pipeline ran from an unreviewed branch yet reached production credentials. Tying those credentials to protected branches and gated environments removes that path. Moving secrets to a vault is good hygiene, but a branch pipeline could still query it with its job token. Config scanning is easy to evade. Signing wouldn't help because the attacker controlled the developer's SSO identity, which the keyless flow trusts."},
{"id":"p5","d":5,"q":"A reporting screen in an older e-commerce admin app lets staff choose a sort column and direction from dropdowns. The code concatenates both values into an ORDER BY clause, and a tester showed it is injectable. The team already converted WHERE clause values to bound parameters last quarter and plans to migrate to a new ORM next year. The database is SQL Server 2019. What should the architect recommend for the sort fields?","o":["Pass the column name and direction as bound parameters, the same way the WHERE clause values were fixed","Escape the values with the database's quoting function and add a WAF rule that blocks SQL keywords in that parameter","Map the incoming values to a server-side allowlist of known column names and directions, and reject anything else","Move the query into a stored procedure that takes the column name as a parameter and builds the statement inside it"],"a":[2],"e":"Identifiers like column names and keywords like ASC/DESC can't be bound as parameters. A bound column name becomes a string literal and the sort silently does nothing. An allowlist mapping is the standard fix. Escaping and WAF rules are bypass-prone. A stored procedure that builds dynamic SQL from the parameter just moves the injection inside the database."},
{"id":"p6","d":5,"q":"A pen test of a customer portal found that a logged-in user can change the number in GET /api/customers/{id} and read other customers' records. The API gateway validates the OAuth access token and checks that it carries the customers:read scope. The development lead proposes switching IDs from sequential integers to random UUIDs before the next audit in six weeks. The portal serves about 80,000 customers and runs on three backend services. What should the architect require?","o":["Move to random UUIDs as proposed, since unguessable identifiers stop the enumeration the testers used","Add per-user rate limits at the gateway on the customers endpoint to slow bulk retrieval of other records","Add a gateway policy that requires a narrower scope, such as customers:read:self, for this endpoint","Check in the backend, per request, that the caller is entitled to the specific record being requested"],"a":[3],"e":"This is broken object level authorization (OWASP API1). The fix is checking ownership or entitlement of the specific object server-side. UUIDs make guessing harder but IDs leak through logs, links, and other endpoints. Rate limits slow abuse without stopping it. A narrower scope is a function-level control; the gateway still can't tell which record belongs to whom."},
{"id":"p7","d":5,"q":"A community forum is adding user comments. Product requires that comments support bold, italics, bulleted lists, and links, entered through a rich text editor that submits HTML. The app is a server-rendered Django site, and a marketing script from a third party loads on every page. A previous incident involved a javascript: URL in a profile field. Which approach BEST prevents stored XSS in comments while meeting the requirement?","o":["Apply contextual HTML output encoding to all comment fields so submitted markup is displayed as text","Run comment HTML through a maintained allowlist sanitizer that limits tags, attributes, and URL schemes","Validate comments on input and reject submissions containing angle brackets or the string javascript:","Deploy a strict Content Security Policy that blocks inline scripts and allows only the site's own script origin"],"a":[1],"e":"Because formatting must render, plain output encoding breaks the feature. An allowlist sanitizer keeps safe tags and strips dangerous attributes and schemes like javascript:. Rejecting angle brackets also breaks the feature and is bypassable through encodings. CSP is good defense in depth, but here it needs allowances for the third-party script and doesn't stop HTML injection itself."},
{"id":"p8","d":5,"q":"A platform team is rolling out a service mesh across 300 microservices written in four languages. The mesh will issue workload certificates and enforce mTLS. The steering committee believes this satisfies the audit finding that 'each service must enforce authorization for the end user making the request.' A customer-facing gateway already validates user JWTs at the edge. Kafka is used for some async flows. What should the architect add to the design?","o":["Mesh authorization policies that list which service accounts may call each service, based on the workload identities","Kubernetes network policies that limit traffic between namespaces so only expected service pairs can connect","Pass a signed or exchanged user token downstream so each service can make user-level authorization decisions","Per-service API keys issued from the secrets manager and checked by each service on each inbound call"],"a":[2],"e":"mTLS and mesh policies prove which workload is calling, not which user the request is for. To meet the finding, user context has to travel downstream in a verifiable form and each service has to make user-level decisions on it. Service-account policies, network policies, and API keys are all workload-level controls and leave the finding open."},
{"id":"p9","d":5,"q":"A developer accidentally made an internal repository public for about six hours. A scan shows the Git history contains a cloud access key for the billing account, a production database password, and a third-party payments API key. The database password is shared by four legacy services, and changing it needs a coordinated restart. The CISO's assistant is asking for a status update for the board. What should the team do FIRST?","o":["Rewrite Git history with a filter tool, force-push, and ask the hosting provider to purge cached views","Revoke and rotate the exposed credentials, starting with the cloud key and the payments key","Enable push protection and pre-commit secret scanning across the organization so this doesn't recur","Pull the cloud and payment provider logs for the six-hour window to see whether the keys were used"],"a":[1],"e":"A secret that was public must be treated as compromised, and automated scraping of public repos happens within minutes. Revoking and rotating comes first. History rewriting doesn't recall copies already taken. Prevention tooling matters but comes after containment. Log review is important and runs right after or in parallel, but waiting on it delays revocation of keys that may be in use."},
{"id":"p10","d":5,"q":"A logistics company has about 120 web applications and APIs, from internal timesheet tools to a customer portal that takes payments. The architect needs a single catalog of security requirements that developers can implement and testers can verify, with stricter requirements for higher-risk apps. The company already follows NIST SSDF at the program level and trains developers on the OWASP Top 10. A separate team owns the two mobile apps. Which source fits BEST?","o":["The OWASP Top 10, mapped to each application's risk tier so higher tiers must show coverage of all ten categories","OWASP ASVS, with a verification level assigned to each application based on its risk tier","NIST SSDF practices, broken into tasks for each development team and tracked in the company's GRC tool","OWASP MASVS, adapted for web applications by removing the iOS and Android specific controls"],"a":[1],"e":"ASVS provides testable, application-level requirements organized by assurance levels, which matches the need. The Top 10 is an awareness list of risk categories, not a requirement set. SSDF describes organizational development practices, not per-application security requirements. MASVS is the mobile counterpart and doesn't fit web and API apps."},
{"id":"p11","d":5,"q":"An insurer's claims portal lets customers upload PDFs, Word files, and photos. A back-end worker converts each upload into a preview image using an office document converter and an image library, then stores the original in object storage. The worker runs on the same VM pool as the claims API and has its database credentials. Upload checks already verify file type by magic bytes, cap size at 20 MB, and run antivirus. What should the architect prioritize?","o":["Add a second antivirus engine and a content disarm and reconstruction step before files reach the worker","Serve stored files from a separate domain with Content-Disposition: attachment and a restrictive content type","Run the converter in an isolated, ephemeral sandbox with no egress and no database credentials","Tighten the file type allowlist to PDF and JPEG only, rejecting Office formats at upload"],"a":[2],"e":"The riskiest component is the converter: complex parsers handling hostile input, running next to the API with database credentials. Isolating it limits the blast radius of a parser exploit. Extra scanning and CDR help but don't contain an exploit that gets through. Separate-domain serving protects users, not the back end. Dropping Office formats may break a business requirement and still leaves image and PDF parsers exposed."},
{"id":"p12","d":5,"q":"A critical remote code execution flaw is announced in a popular logging module. The vendor patch is expected in ten days. The company has about 60 Java services across three business units; some are internet-facing behind a CDN with a managed WAF and others are internal. The module is often pulled in as a transitive dependency, and the AppSec team doesn't know which services include it. SCA runs in CI but its results go to each team separately. What should happen FIRST?","o":["Query existing SCA and SBOM data across repos and running images to find affected services","Deploy a WAF virtual patch in blocking mode on the CDN for all internet-facing hostnames","Enable RASP agents on internet-facing Java services to block exploitation at runtime until the patch arrives","Ask each business unit to confirm within 48 hours whether their services use the logging module"],"a":[0],"e":"You can't prioritize or shield what you can't find, and transitive use means teams often don't know. Centralizing existing SCA and SBOM data scopes exposure fastest and most reliably. A WAF virtual patch is a strong next step for the exposed services but is blind to internal ones. RASP rollout takes time and needs scoping too. Asking teams relies on the knowledge they lack."},
{"id":"p13","d":5,"q":"A SaaS HR platform keeps 2,000 tenants in a shared PostgreSQL schema with a tenant_id column. Two past bugs leaked data across tenants when developers wrote raw SQL reports and forgot the filter. One enterprise prospect is asking for a dedicated database. The team uses an ORM for most queries and has a small DBA group. Engineering leadership wants a fix that doesn't depend on each developer remembering. Which design BEST reduces cross-tenant leakage across the platform?","o":["Add a global tenant filter in the ORM so queries made through it automatically include the tenant condition","Enable PostgreSQL row-level security keyed to a per-session tenant setting, alongside the app filters","Move each tenant to its own database, starting with the enterprise prospect, to give physical separation","Encrypt each tenant's rows with a tenant-specific key held in a KMS so other tenants' data is unreadable"],"a":[1],"e":"Row-level security enforces the boundary in the database, so raw SQL that forgets the filter still can't return other tenants' rows. The ORM filter misses exactly the raw-SQL path that caused the leaks. A database per tenant for 2,000 tenants is a major cost and operations change; it may suit the prospect but isn't the platform fix. Per-tenant encryption doesn't help when the application itself decrypts whatever the query returns."},
{"id":"p14","d":5,"q":"A bank's web app runs at app.example.com and sets its session cookie with Domain=example.com, Secure, HttpOnly, and SameSite=Lax. Marketing microsites on other example.com subdomains are built and hosted by an outside agency with its own deployment access. Security found that a compromised microsite could set cookies that the main app would accept. Session lifetime is already 15 minutes idle. Which change BEST addresses this?","o":["Rename the cookie with the __Host- prefix, which requires Secure, Path=/, and no Domain attribute","Change the cookie to SameSite=Strict so it is only sent on requests that originate from the main app","Shorten the absolute session lifetime to one hour and rotate the session ID after each sensitive action","Add a CSP frame-ancestors directive and HSTS with includeSubDomains on the main app's responses"],"a":[0],"e":"The issue is subdomains being able to read or overwrite (toss) the session cookie. The __Host- prefix locks the cookie to the exact host and browsers reject attempts by other subdomains to set it. SameSite is about cross-site requests, and sibling subdomains count as same-site, so Strict doesn't help. Shorter sessions reduce exposure without fixing scope. HSTS and frame-ancestors address other attacks."},
{"id":"p15","d":5,"q":"An online retailer's checkout page loads six third-party scripts. To meet PCI DSS v4 script requirements, the team must authorize each script, assure its integrity, and detect unauthorized changes. One analytics vendor serves its script from a CDN, updates it without notice every few weeks, and has confirmed in the contract that customers may self-host a fixed version. Product only needs the vendor's basic page tracking. What is the BEST approach for this script?","o":["Add a Subresource Integrity hash to the vendor's CDN URL and update the hash whenever the script changes","Allow the vendor's CDN domain in the page's Content Security Policy and block other script sources","Self-host a reviewed, pinned copy with an SRI hash and update it only through change control","Load the script in a sandboxed iframe on the checkout page and monitor its network calls"],"a":[2],"e":"Since the contract allows self-hosting and only basic tracking is needed, a reviewed pinned copy gives authorization and integrity, with changes going through review. SRI on the vendor URL breaks every time the vendor silently updates. A CSP domain allowlist doesn't detect content changes on that domain. A sandboxed iframe may break the analytics and is more work than self-hosting a static copy."},
{"id":"p16","d":5,"q":"A retailer is launching an LLM-based support assistant that can call internal order APIs as tools. In the prototype, tool calls run under a service account that can read all orders. Customers type free text, and order notes written by other customers and staff are fed back into the model's context. Legal wants the launch before the holiday season, and the vendor offers a prompt-injection classifier as an add-on. Which control BEST limits what a successful prompt injection could expose?","o":["A system prompt that instructs the model to discuss only the authenticated customer's orders and to refuse other requests","Executing tool calls with a token scoped to the signed-in customer, so the APIs return only that customer's orders","The vendor's prompt-injection classifier on customer messages, blocking inputs that score above a tuned threshold","An output filter that scans assistant replies for other customers' names, addresses, and order numbers before sending"],"a":[1],"e":"Prompt injection can't be reliably prevented in the model, and order notes give an indirect injection path that an input classifier on customer messages won't see. The durable control is limiting the model's authority: if tools act with the customer's own scoped authorization, a hijacked model still can't fetch other data. System prompt rules can be overridden. Output filters are leaky and catch only patterns they know."},
{"id":"p17","d":5,"q":"An architect is designing an internal web framework that 60 product teams will adopt over the next year. Past audits found teams forgetting to enable CSRF protection and output encoding. Some teams build legitimate edge cases, such as webhook receivers that can't use CSRF tokens. Senior engineers warn that if the framework is too rigid, teams will fork it. Which approach BEST improves security outcomes?","o":["Ship security features as well-documented helper functions in the framework and require teams to call them in code review","Turn protections on by default with narrow per-route opt-outs that are logged and flagged for a security review","Lock the protections on with no override, so the framework rejects routes that try to disable CSRF or encoding","Keep the framework neutral and fund annual secure coding training so teams know when to enable each protection"],"a":[1],"e":"Secure-by-default with visible, reviewable exceptions gives protection without forgetting and still handles real edge cases like webhooks. Opt-in helpers depend on the same memory that failed in audits. A hard lock pushes teams with real edge cases to fork or work around the framework. Training helps but doesn't change the default behavior that caused the findings."},
{"id":"p18","d":5,"q":"A company's API gateway validates OAuth tokens and then forwards requests to backend services with an X-User-Id header. Backends trust the header and skip their own checks. The same backends are also called directly by nightly batch jobs inside the VPC and by a partner over a site-to-site VPN. A recent internal pen test spoofed the header from a compromised build server. Which change should the architect prioritize?","o":["Restrict backend security groups so only the gateway's subnets can reach them, and move batch jobs behind the gateway","Have backends validate a signed token themselves, checking issuer, audience, and expiry, and authorize from its claims","Add mTLS between the gateway and the backends so only a client holding the gateway's certificate can connect","Move all authorization decisions into the gateway using a central policy engine, so backends need no logic of their own"],"a":[1],"e":"The weakness is trusting a forgeable header based on network position, and there are several paths into the backends. Having each backend verify a signed token removes that trust. Network restrictions still trust location and break the partner path. mTLS to the gateway helps but leaves backends trusting whatever identity the gateway asserts in a plain header. A gateway policy engine lacks object-level context and keeps the backends blind."},
{"id":"p19","d":5,"q":"A media company has about 200 development teams and a central AppSec group of six. Two years ago it ran a security champions program that faded within months because champions had no time set aside and their managers kept pulling them back to feature work. The CISO has budget for either three more AppSec hires or tooling. Developers say security reviews are the slowest part of delivery. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Use the budget for three AppSec hires focused on design reviews for the highest-risk teams, with others self-serving","Relaunch champions with protected time agreed by engineering leadership and clear goals, backed by paved-road tooling","Require an external penetration test before each major release, funded from the budget, to shift review load off AppSec","Buy an enterprise SAST and SCA platform with pipeline gates so automated checks replace most manual security reviews"],"a":[1],"e":"The program failed for a known, fixable reason: no time allocation or leadership backing. Fixing that, plus tooling that makes the secure path easy, is the only option that scales to 200 teams. Three hires still leave a 6-to-200 ratio. External tests per release add delay and cost. Tooling helps but can't replace design-level judgment inside teams."},
{"id":"p20","d":5,"q":"A Java service receives native Java serialized objects from a logistics partner over an existing integration. The partner says it can't move to JSON for at least nine months. The partner suffered a network breach last year, and the payload is already wrapped in an HMAC using a key both sides share. The service runs on Java 17 and pulls in several libraries with known gadget chains. What should the architect require now?","o":["Rely on the existing HMAC by verifying it before deserializing, since only the partner holds the shared key","Remove the libraries known to contain gadget chains from the classpath and rescan with SCA each release","Apply a strict deserialization filter that allows only the expected classes, and plan the JSON migration","Deploy a WAF rule that detects serialized Java object signatures with known gadget class names in the request"],"a":[2],"e":"An allowlist filter (ObjectInputFilter, from JEP 290) stops unexpected classes from being instantiated whatever the gadget chain. Verifying an HMAC before deserializing is a sound pattern, but here the partner was breached, so a signed malicious payload is plausible. Removing gadget libraries is whack-a-mole because new chains keep appearing. WAF signatures only catch known chains and are easy to obfuscate."},
{"id":"p21","d":5,"q":"A company moved to Terraform and Amazon EKS last year. Developers run terraform apply and kubectl from their laptops with long-lived admin credentials. A SOC 2 Type II audit window opens in four months, and auditors will look for change control evidence. The platform team already scans Terraform in pull requests, but nothing stops a developer from applying unmerged code. Which change BEST addresses the core risk?","o":["Run policy-as-code checks in a pre-apply hook on developer laptops so noncompliant plans are blocked locally","Require MFA on the admin credentials and record terminal sessions when developers run apply against production","Make the pipeline the only identity with production write access, with a logged break-glass path for humans","Expand pull request scanning to include Kubernetes manifests and container images so issues are caught before merge"],"a":[2],"e":"The core risk is that production changes bypass review. Removing human write access and deploying only through the pipeline makes the reviewed path the only path and produces audit evidence. Local hooks can be skipped. MFA and session recording add accountability but still allow unreviewed changes. Broader PR scanning doesn't help when people can apply code that never went through a PR."},
{"id":"p22","d":5,"q":"A service receives XML purchase orders from 40 partners. It verifies an XML digital signature on each document, then validates it against an XSD and maps it to internal objects. The parser is configured with library defaults, and the XSD files reference external schema locations. Documents are capped at 2 MB. A tester reports the server made outbound requests to an unknown host during a test. Which change should the architect require?","o":["Configure the parser to disable DTDs, external entity resolution, and external schema fetching","Verify the XML signature before doing further processing so unsigned or altered documents are dropped","Validate each document strictly against a locally pinned copy of the XSD before mapping it to objects","Lower the size cap to 256 KB and set a parsing time limit to protect against entity expansion attacks"],"a":[0],"e":"Outbound requests during parsing point to XXE or external resource fetching, which is fixed in parser configuration. Signature verification itself needs the document parsed first, and signature wrapping attacks exist, so it doesn't prevent XXE. Local XSD pinning helps one fetch path but not entity resolution in the parse. Size and time limits reduce expansion attacks but not external entity reads."},
{"id":"p23","d":5,"q":"A fintech web app lets users enter a URL so the server fetches a profile image from it. The app runs on cloud VMs that still allow the older metadata service version, and the instance role can read a storage bucket with customer statements. Product has refused to replace the feature with file uploads. The team has a WAF and a URL validation function that checks hostnames against a denylist of private ranges. What BEST reduces the impact of SSRF here?","o":["Strengthen the denylist to cover IPv6, decimal, and octal encodings of private and link-local addresses","Add a WAF rule that blocks requests containing 169.254.169.254 or metadata hostnames in parameters","Enforce token-based metadata access with a hop limit of 1 and route fetches via an isolated egress proxy","Move the statements bucket behind a VPC endpoint policy so it can only be read from the application subnet"],"a":[2],"e":"Hostname denylists are bypassed through DNS rebinding and redirects, and WAF patterns miss encoded forms. Enforcing token-based metadata access blocks the common credential-theft path, and an isolated proxy that resolves and blocks internal ranges contains the fetch itself. A VPC endpoint policy doesn't help because the SSRF request originates from the app's own subnet with the app's role."},
{"id":"p24","d":5,"q":"A health insurer is building a member mobile app. To avoid frequent logins, developers plan to store the user's password on the device and silently re-authenticate. The identity provider supports OAuth 2.0 with PKCE and refresh token rotation. Members often reuse passwords across sites, and the security team needs the ability to cut off a lost device without forcing a password reset. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Store the password in the iOS Keychain and Android Keystore with hardware backing, and re-authenticate silently","Use OAuth with PKCE and keep a rotating refresh token in hardware-backed storage, revocable per device ID","Encrypt the password with AES-256 using a key derived from the device ID, then keep it in app preferences","Store a salted hash of the password locally and compare it at launch, so the app unlocks without network"],"a":[1],"e":"A device-scoped, rotating refresh token keeps users signed in, can be revoked for one lost device, and doesn't expose a reusable password. Keystore storage protects the password better but still keeps a credential that works on other sites and can only be cut off by a reset. A device-ID derived key is easy to recover. A local hash doesn't produce anything the server will accept and enables offline guessing."},
{"id":"p25","d":5,"q":"A code review finds that an internal library encrypts customer PII fields with AES-CBC using a static IV and no integrity check. The developer who wrote it left the company, and 14 services use it. The data team wants minimal changes because the fields are indexed and searched. A KMS is already available. The privacy officer has classified the fields as high sensitivity. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Keep CBC but generate a random IV per message, storing it with the ciphertext, to fix the static IV issue","Add an HMAC over the ciphertext using the same AES key, and verify it before decryption in the library","Switch to a vetted library's AEAD mode with KMS-managed keys, and use blind indexes for field search","Commission an external cryptographer to review the current library and document its acceptable uses"],"a":[2],"e":"An AEAD mode from a vetted library (such as AES-GCM via Tink) gives confidentiality and integrity, KMS handles keys, and blind indexes keep search working without deterministic encryption of the field. A random IV fixes one issue but leaves no integrity, so padding oracle attacks remain. Reusing the encryption key for the MAC is poor practice and still homegrown. A review documents the problem without fixing it."},
{"id":"p26","d":5,"q":"A company piloted a SAST tool on its flagship application and got 4,000 findings. Developers have stopped opening the reports, and two team leads called the tool 'noise.' The vendor contract runs three years. The application has about a million lines of code, and leadership wants a visible win within a quarter. What should the architect do NEXT?","o":["Gate new code on a small set of high-confidence rules and baseline the legacy findings for later triage","Configure the pipeline to fail builds on all high and critical findings so developers have to address them","Hire contractors to triage the full 4,000-finding backlog first, then hand verified results to developers","Run a bake-off with two other SAST vendors to find a tool with a lower false-positive rate on this codebase"],"a":[0],"e":"Starting with high-confidence rules on new code gives developers accurate, actionable results and stops new debt, while the backlog is triaged separately. Failing builds on the full set would halt delivery over noise. Triaging the backlog first delays any visible win and doesn't fix the experience going forward. Switching vendors in a three-year contract is costly and likely to recreate the same issue without tuning."},
{"id":"p27","d":5,"q":"A merchant is redesigning its web checkout and wants to minimize PCI DSS scope. The payment provider offers a full-page redirect, hosted card fields in iframes styled by the merchant, or a JavaScript library that collects card data in the merchant's own page and tokenizes it. The brand team insists the checkout stay on the merchant's domain with consistent styling. The site runs on a shared marketing CMS. Which option fits BEST?","o":["The full-page redirect to the provider, since card data stays off the merchant's own site","The provider's iframe-hosted card fields, styled to match the site and embedded on the merchant's page","The provider's JavaScript library that tokenizes card data in the merchant's own page before submission","Collect card data on the merchant's server and tokenize it immediately through the provider's API"],"a":[1],"e":"Hosted iframe fields keep card data in the provider's context, supporting the smallest SAQ scope, while meeting the brand requirement to stay on the merchant's domain. A redirect has similarly small scope but leaves the domain, which the brand team rejected. A JavaScript library running in the merchant's page puts the page in scope at a higher level. Server-side collection brings the merchant's systems fully into scope."},
{"id":"p28","d":5,"q":"Developers on a claims platform want to log full HTTP requests, including headers and bodies, to speed debugging. Requests carry bearer tokens, claimant medical details, and bank account numbers. The incident response team says it has struggled in past investigations without payload data. Logs flow to a SIEM retained for a year, and the company operates under GDPR. Which approach BEST balances these needs?","o":["Log full requests and rely on encryption at rest and role-based access in the SIEM to protect the sensitive fields","Log full requests and apply regex masking in the SIEM ingestion pipeline to strip tokens and account numbers","Log allowlisted fields with correlation IDs, and keep redacted payloads in a short-lived, restricted debug store","Log request metadata only, such as method, path, status, and timing, and leave bodies out of logging"],"a":[2],"e":"Allowlisting fields keeps tokens and special category data out of long-term logs, while a short-retention, access-controlled debug path meets the investigation need. Encryption doesn't fix over-collection or a year of retention under GDPR. Masking at the SIEM means secrets have already traveled through the log pipeline and regexes miss formats. Metadata only ignores the incident response team's documented need."},
{"id":"p29","d":5,"q":"A company acquired a smaller firm whose claims application processes sensitive customer data and must stay in production. There are no automated tests, no architecture documents, and the original developers have left. One contractor who knows the system is under contract for 30 more days. The acquirer's WAF and SIEM can be extended to it next quarter. The board wants a risk picture at next month's meeting. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Run full SAST and DAST scans against the codebase and staging environment to build a vulnerability baseline","Put the application behind the corporate WAF and SIEM ahead of schedule to get compensating controls in place","Work with the contractor to document architecture, data flows, and interfaces, then threat model the system","Begin scoping a rewrite on the acquirer's standard stack, since the code has no tests and unclear ownership"],"a":[2],"e":"The scarcest resource is the contractor's knowledge, which disappears in 30 days. Capturing architecture and data flows now enables a threat model, a board-ready risk picture, and better-targeted scans and controls later. Scans can run anytime and are hard to interpret without context. Accelerating WAF and SIEM is useful but can wait the few weeks. A rewrite decision is premature without understanding the system."},
{"id":"p30","d":5,"q":"A hospital network is building an in-house mobile app for patients to view lab results. The app must protect data at rest and in transit, resist reverse engineering and tampering, and be tested against clear criteria by an outside lab. The web portal already uses OWASP ASVS Level 2. The procurement team also mentions NIST guidance it uses to vet third-party apps on staff phones. Which standard BEST defines the requirements and test procedures?","o":["OWASP ASVS Level 3, applied to the app's API calls and local storage the same way as the portal","OWASP MASVS for requirements, with the MASTG for test procedures, including the resilience controls","NIST SP 800-163, the guidance for vetting mobile applications, applied to the in-house app during testing","The OWASP Mobile Top 10, with each category mapped to a test case in the outside lab's statement of work"],"a":[1],"e":"MASVS defines mobile app security requirements, including a resilience category for anti-tampering and reverse engineering, and MASTG gives the matching test procedures. ASVS targets web apps and doesn't cover mobile platform storage or resilience well. SP 800-163 is aimed at organizations vetting apps they deploy, not at defining requirements for building one. The Mobile Top 10 is an awareness list, not a testable requirement set."},
{"id":"p31","d":5,"q":"A manufacturer has 45 internal web applications. Most have their own login screens and password tables, with hashing that ranges from bcrypt to unsalted MD5. Ten are commercial products that support SAML, eight are legacy apps the business won't fund changes to, and the rest are in-house. The company already runs an identity provider with MFA for email and VPN. Which approach MOST reduces credential risk across the portfolio?","o":["Set a common password policy and require each in-house team to migrate its stored hashes to bcrypt or Argon2","Point the apps at Active Directory through LDAP binds so there is one password store with central lockout","Federate the apps to the existing IdP over SAML or OIDC, with an identity-aware proxy for legacy apps","Roll out an enterprise password manager so users have unique, strong passwords for each of the 45 applications"],"a":[2],"e":"Federation removes local password handling and brings MFA and central control to each app, and an identity-aware proxy covers the legacy apps that can't change. Hash migration fixes storage but leaves 45 separate credential stores without MFA. LDAP binds centralize the directory but the apps still receive raw passwords and get no MFA. A password manager helps users but leaves the weak app-side storage in place."},
{"id":"p32","d":5,"q":"A critical CVE is published for an open-source image library used by a public photo upload feature. The maintainers haven't responded in two weeks, and no patch exists. Analysis shows the flaw is in an optional TIFF decoder, and the product only needs JPEG and PNG. Replacing the library would take about two months of work. The release train is weekly. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Fork the library, write and test a fix for the decoder, and maintain the fork until the maintainers release a patch","Disable the TIFF decoder through configuration, reject TIFF uploads, and record a time-bound risk exception","Start the two-month effort to replace the library with an alternative that has an active maintainer base","Add a WAF rule that inspects uploaded files for the malformed TIFF headers described in the public advisory"],"a":[1],"e":"The vulnerable code path is optional and unneeded, so turning it off removes exposure quickly at low cost, and a dated exception keeps the residual risk visible. Forking creates a long-term maintenance burden for a decoder the product doesn't use. Replacement may be wise later but leaves two months of exposure. WAF inspection of binary uploads is fragile and easy to evade."},
{"id":"p33","d":5,"q":"A Node.js API binds incoming JSON bodies directly onto ORM models. A tester promoted himself to admin by adding \"role\":\"admin\" to a profile update request. The team proposes a denylist that strips role, isAdmin, and tenantId before saving. The data model has 140 entities and grows each sprint. An API gateway already validates JWTs and enforces request size limits. What should the architect require?","o":["Adopt the proposed denylist, centralized in a middleware so it applies to each endpoint consistently","Bind each endpoint to an explicit request DTO that allows only the fields that endpoint may change","Add gateway schema validation that checks the type and format of the known fields in each request","Add a post-save audit check that alerts when a user's role or tenant changes outside the admin console"],"a":[1],"e":"Explicit per-endpoint allowlists (DTOs) mean new or sensitive fields can't be set unless someone deliberately allows them. A denylist fails open as the model grows. Type and format checks on known fields don't stop extra fields being sent. Post-save alerting detects abuse after it happens rather than preventing it."},
{"id":"p34","d":5,"q":"Developers at a software company are already using public AI coding assistants on personal accounts. The CISO is concerned about two things: proprietary code being retained or used for training by the provider, and insecure code suggestions reaching production. Legal has reviewed one vendor's enterprise tier, which offers zero data retention and admin controls. The company already runs SAST, SCA, and mandatory peer review. What is the BEST approach?","o":["Ban AI coding assistants by policy and block the known provider domains at the web proxy","License the reviewed enterprise tier, and apply the same SAST, SCA, and review gates to code whatever its origin","Allow the assistants but require developers to tag AI-generated code so the security team can review it separately","Allow assistants generally and deploy DLP rules that stop source files from being uploaded to the provider endpoints"],"a":[1],"e":"A sanctioned enterprise tier addresses retention and training through contract and settings, and existing gates catch insecure code no matter who or what wrote it. A ban drives use further into personal accounts. Tagging is unreliable and doesn't scale to a central review. DLP that blocks source uploads breaks the tools while leaving personal-account use and insecure suggestions unaddressed."},
{"id":"p35","d":5,"q":"A code review of a web app's password reset flow finds that tokens are generated from the current timestamp. The developer's proposed fix is to build the token from a SHA-256 hash of the timestamp combined with the user ID. Reset links are emailed and currently stay valid for 24 hours. The app has about 2 million accounts, and the user ID is shown in profile URLs. What should the architect require?","o":["Use the SHA-256 approach as proposed, since hashing the timestamp with the user ID makes the token much harder to guess","Generate tokens with a version 1 UUID, which combines a timestamp with the server's node ID for uniqueness","Generate tokens from a CSPRNG with at least 128 bits, store only their hash, and make them single-use and short-lived","Generate a 32-character token from the language's standard random function, seeded at startup, and email it"],"a":[2],"e":"Tokens need to be unpredictable, which takes a cryptographically secure generator, and storing only hashes plus single use and short expiry limits misuse. Hashing a known user ID with a guessable timestamp is still predictable. Version 1 UUIDs are time-based and largely predictable. Standard non-cryptographic PRNGs can have their state recovered from outputs."},
{"id":"p36","d":5,"q":"A team runs about 300 serverless functions. Database credentials and API keys are set as plaintext environment variables in Terraform, which means they also appear in state files and in the console for anyone with read access to function configuration. Compliance now requires database credentials to rotate every 30 days without redeploying functions. Cold start latency is a known concern for the checkout functions. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Encrypt the environment variables with a KMS key and decrypt them during function initialization","Grant each function's role only its own secrets in a secrets manager, fetched at init and cached briefly","Fetch each secret from the secrets manager on each invocation so functions see rotated values right away","Move the secrets into the function's container image at build time and rebuild the image when they rotate"],"a":[1],"e":"A secrets manager with per-function access supports automatic rotation without redeploys, and caching at init with a short TTL keeps cold starts and API calls low while picking up rotated values. KMS-encrypted environment variables still need a redeploy to rotate. Fetching on every invocation adds latency and cost and risks throttling. Baking secrets into images spreads them through registries and needs rebuilds."},
{"id":"p37","d":5,"q":"A CRM exports contact lists to CSV for sales staff, who open them in Excel. A tester entered a company name beginning with =HYPERLINK( and the formula ran when the file was opened. Legitimate data includes international phone numbers that start with + and balance adjustments that start with -. The export is used by 900 staff, and the CRM vendor's UI already HTML-encodes fields on screen. Which approach BEST fixes this?","o":["Reject input at entry that begins with =, +, -, or @ in free-text fields across the CRM","On export, prefix cells starting with =, +, -, @, tab, or CR with a single quote, and quote each field","HTML-encode field values in the export, the same way the CRM already encodes them on screen","Serve the export with a text/plain content type and Content-Disposition: attachment to stop automatic execution"],"a":[1],"e":"Neutralizing risky leading characters at export time stops spreadsheet formula injection while keeping phone numbers and negative amounts intact as text. Rejecting input would block legitimate data. HTML encoding doesn't affect how Excel parses formulas. Content headers control how the browser handles the download, not how Excel interprets cells once the file is opened."},
{"id":"p38","d":5,"q":"A bank exposes a GraphQL API used only by its own iOS, Android, and web apps; no third parties call it. Attackers recently sent deeply nested queries that spiked database load, and separately sent single requests containing hundreds of aliased login mutations to guess passwords. The gateway rate-limits by IP at 100 requests per minute. Introspection is already disabled in production. Which control BEST addresses both attacks?","o":["Add query depth limits and cost analysis that reject operations above a calculated complexity budget","Use persisted queries so the server runs only the operation IDs registered by the bank's own apps","Lower the per-IP rate limit to 20 requests per minute and add a CAPTCHA on repeated login failures","Disable aliases in the GraphQL schema and cap request body size at 4 KB at the API gateway"],"a":[1],"e":"Since only first-party clients call the API, persisted queries let the server run only known operations, which stops arbitrary nesting and alias batching in one control. Depth and cost limits are the right fit for public GraphQL APIs but don't handle alias-based brute force by themselves. Per-request rate limits don't count aliased operations inside one request. Blocking aliases and capping size helps one attack and is easy to work around for the other."},
{"id":"p39","d":5,"q":"A pen test of a new B2B portal found that users from one customer could see another customer's invoices because the design never defined how customer boundaries would be enforced. Fixing it now requires changing the data model and three services, two weeks before launch. The portal team used SAST in CI, DAST in staging, and peer code review. Leadership asks which activity, added to the process, would have caught this most cheaply.","o":["Threat modeling at design review, focused on trust boundaries and customer data separation","SAST rules in CI that flag database queries missing a customer ID filter in the WHERE clause","Authenticated DAST in staging with test accounts from two customers, run before each release","A secure code review checklist item asking reviewers to confirm customer filtering in data access code"],"a":[0],"e":"This is a design flaw: the isolation model was never defined. Threat modeling at design is where it would surface, before any code exists, so it's the cheapest point to fix. SAST rules can't tell what a correct filter is when the design doesn't define one. Two-customer DAST can detect it, but only after the code is built. A review checklist catches individual lapses, not a missing design decision."},
{"id":"p40","d":5,"q":"A team deploys code to production behind feature flags weeks before features launch. A reviewer finds that the flag is checked only in the React front end, so the new bulk-export API endpoints are live and callable by any authenticated user. Security testing of each feature is scheduled for the week before its flag is turned on. The flag service is a SaaS product the company already pays for. Which change should the architect require?","o":["Hide the new endpoints by giving them non-obvious paths until the feature is launched to customers","Keep the plan of testing each feature in the week before its flag is enabled, and add a sign-off gate","Enforce the flag server-side in the API, and security test dark-launched code before it deploys","Move the flag check from the React app into the API gateway's routing rules for the new paths"],"a":[2],"e":"Deployed code is attack surface whether a flag is on or not, so the flag must be enforced on the server and the code tested before it ships. Obscure paths are discoverable through the front-end bundle. Testing just before launch leaves weeks of exposure. Gateway routing rules help but separate the flag logic from the service and are easy to drift out of sync with the flag service."},
{"id":"p41","d":5,"q":"An architect is choosing runtime testing for a QA environment. The platform has 25 Java services and 15 Go services. QA runs automated functional tests that cover about 70% of features. The candidate IAST product supports Java but not Go. Leadership wants fewer false positives than the current SAST tool and results within the existing test cycle. Which plan fits BEST?","o":["Deploy IAST agents on all 40 services so the functional tests drive coverage across the whole platform","Run DAST against all services in QA and drop IAST, so one tool covers both languages the same way","Deploy IAST on the Java services driven by the functional tests, and run DAST against the Go services","Expand SAST to cover the Go services and use its results in place of runtime testing in the QA cycle"],"a":[2],"e":"IAST gives accurate, code-level findings with few false positives where it's supported, and the existing functional tests drive it inside the cycle. DAST fills the gap for Go. IAST can't instrument Go services with this product. DAST alone gives up IAST's precision on the Java majority. More SAST doesn't meet the goal of fewer false positives or runtime coverage."},
{"id":"p42","d":5,"q":"A SPA at app.example.com calls an API at api.example.com using cookie-based sessions. Two partner portals on partner-a.com and partner-b.net also call the API from the browser. Developers couldn't get a wildcard origin to work with credentials, so the API now reflects whatever Origin header arrives and sets Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true. The marketing team hosts landing pages on other example.com subdomains through a third-party builder. What should the architect require?","o":["Match Origin against an explicit list of the three known origins, echo only exact matches, set Vary: Origin","Allow origins that match a regex for example.com subdomains plus the two partner domains, keeping credentials enabled","Switch the API to bearer tokens in the Authorization header and return Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * without credentials","Keep reflecting the Origin header and add CSRF tokens to state-changing requests to block cross-site abuse"],"a":[0],"e":"Reflecting any origin with credentials lets any site read authenticated responses. An exact-match allowlist of the three real origins fixes it with minimal change. A subdomain regex trusts the third-party-hosted marketing pages. Moving to bearer tokens is a valid design in a different situation but is a larger rework than needed. CSRF tokens don't stop cross-origin reads that CORS reflection allows."},
{"id":"p43","d":5,"q":"A company with about 500 services wants proof at deploy time that each artifact was built from reviewed source by the official CI system and not altered afterward. The build toolchains include several that aren't deterministic, and the platform team has one year and a small budget. Releases are already signed by a release manager's key, and SBOMs are generated for each build. Which approach BEST meets the goal?","o":["Rely on the existing release signing, adding a check at deploy that the release manager's signature is valid","Generate SLSA provenance on a hardened build platform and verify it, with the source commit, at deploy admission","Adopt reproducible builds and have an independent system rebuild each artifact and compare hashes before deploy","Attach the SBOM to each artifact and check at deploy that its component list matches the approved baseline"],"a":[1],"e":"Signed provenance from a hardened builder records what source and build produced each artifact, and admission checks enforce it at deploy. That fits 500 services in a year. Release signing shows who approved, not how or from what it was built. Reproducible builds are stronger but impractical with nondeterministic toolchains on this timeline. An SBOM lists contents but doesn't prove build origin."},
{"id":"p44","d":5,"q":"A tester bypassed a React app's input checks with an intercepting proxy and submitted oversized and malformed values that crashed a back-end job. The same API also serves a mobile app and a partner integration. The front-end team proposes signing each request in JavaScript so tampered requests are rejected. The mobile team already uses certificate pinning. What should the architect require?","o":["Adopt request signing in the SPA with a key bundled in the build, rotating it with each front-end release","Enforce the same validation server-side at the API boundary using shared schemas, for all clients alike","Extend certificate pinning to the web client through a service worker so proxies can't intercept traffic","Add WAF rules that enforce maximum lengths and allowed characters on the affected API parameters"],"a":[1],"e":"Anything running on the client can be bypassed, and the API has three different clients. Server-side validation from shared schemas protects all of them. A signing key shipped in JavaScript can be extracted. Pinning can be bypassed on a device the attacker controls and browsers don't support it this way. WAF rules are a coarse backstop and drift from the real data rules."},
{"id":"p45","d":5,"q":"An insurer plans to replace its claims system with a COTS product that will be heavily customized. The vendor won't share source code. Contract signing is six weeks away. Procurement suggests running a vulnerability scan against the vendor's public demo instance, but the vendor's terms prohibit testing it. The vendor holds an ISO 27001 certificate. Which approach gives the MOST useful assurance before signing?","o":["Require source code escrow with a third-party agent, released if the vendor stops supporting the product","Scan the public demo anyway with a light, non-intrusive profile to get an independent view of the product","Contract for SDLC evidence, an SBOM, patch SLAs, and rights to test the insurer's own configured instance","Accept the ISO 27001 certificate as evidence and request its Statement of Applicability for review"],"a":[2],"e":"Contractual evidence and testing rights give assurance about the actual product and the insurer's configured deployment, and they can be set before signing. Escrow protects continuity, not security. Scanning the demo breaches the terms and the demo doesn't reflect the customized build. ISO 27001 covers the vendor's management system, not the product's code quality."},
{"id":"p46","d":5,"q":"A mid-sized software vendor receives vulnerability reports through its sales inbox, support tickets, and occasionally social media. Some reports have sat unanswered for months, and one researcher went public out of frustration. The product security team is two people with no triage process. Marketing proposes launching a public bug bounty with cash rewards to show the company takes security seriously. What should the vendor do FIRST?","o":["Launch the public bug bounty on a managed platform that handles researcher payments and initial triage","Publish a disclosure policy with scope, safe harbor, and a security.txt contact, plus clear triage SLAs","Start a private, invite-only bug bounty with a handful of researchers to test the team's capacity","Register as a CVE Numbering Authority so the company can assign identifiers to its own vulnerabilities"],"a":[1],"e":"The immediate problem is no clear intake and no process, so a disclosure policy with a defined channel, safe harbor, and response targets comes first. A public bounty would flood a two-person team that can't yet triage. A private bounty is a sensible later step once the process works. CNA status helps with advisories but doesn't fix intake or response."},
{"id":"p47","d":5,"q":"A beverage retailer's loyalty app asks for full date of birth to confirm users are over 21 before showing alcohol promotions. Marketing also wants to send birthday offers, and the analytics team wants to segment by exact age. The app operates in several US states with privacy laws, and a breach last year exposed a different company's loyalty data. Which design BEST supports data minimization?","o":["Keep full date of birth, encrypted at rest with KMS, decryptable only by marketing","Store a verified over-21 flag, and with separate consent keep only birth month and day for birthday offers","Tokenize the date of birth through a vault so analytics and marketing work with tokens rather than the value","Keep full date of birth with a two-year retention limit and role-based access restricted to marketing analysts"],"a":[1],"e":"Minimization means collecting and keeping only what each purpose needs: a flag answers the age check, and month and day serve birthday offers with consent. Encryption and tokenization protect the full date but still keep more than needed. A retention limit and access controls reduce exposure without reducing what's collected. Exact-age analytics isn't a need that justifies keeping the full date."},
{"id":"p48","d":5,"q":"A company's internal npm packages use unscoped names like billing-utils. The build agents are configured with both the internal registry and public npm. An attacker published billing-utils with a higher version number on public npm, and it was pulled in when a developer added a new internal dependency and regenerated the lockfile. The company already runs SCA. Which control BEST prevents a repeat?","o":["Commit lockfiles with integrity hashes and use npm ci in builds so resolved versions are fixed","Add an SCA policy that flags packages with known malicious indicators before they are installed","Move internal packages to a scope resolved only from the internal registry, and claim it on npm","Block public packages published in the last 30 days from being installed by the build agents"],"a":[2],"e":"The root cause is ambiguous resolution between registries. A scope bound to the internal registry, and claimed on the public one, removes that ambiguity. Lockfiles help but the attack happened when a new lockfile was generated. SCA malicious-package feeds lag new uploads. An age cooldown helps against fresh typosquats but an attacker can publish early and wait."},
{"id":"p49","d":5,"q":"A web app calls an orders service, which calls a payments service, which calls an external bank API on the user's behalf. Today the user's access token, issued with a broad audience, is forwarded unchanged through each hop. A threat model notes that a compromised orders service could replay that token to any other internal API. The identity provider supports OAuth 2.0 token exchange. Which design BEST preserves user context while limiting this risk?","o":["Keep forwarding the original token and have each service check its audience claim","Exchange the token at each hop for one limited to the next service's audience and reduced scope","Have each service call downstream with its own client credentials token and pass the user ID in a header","Add mTLS between the services so only known workloads can connect, and keep forwarding the original token"],"a":[1],"e":"Token exchange gives each hop a token valid only for the next service with reduced scope, and records who is acting for the user. A broad audience check passes everywhere, so replay still works. Client credentials lose verifiable user context and make the header forgeable. mTLS limits which workloads connect but the forwarded token can still be replayed by a compromised but legitimate workload."},
{"id":"p50","d":5,"q":"A company's container images are built on a full Ubuntu base with compilers and package managers left in. The image scanner reports around 400 CVEs per image, and teams have stopped looking. The platform team has capacity for one initiative this quarter. Production clusters already enforce read-only root filesystems for about half the workloads. Which initiative MOST reduces vulnerability exposure?","o":["Patch critical and high CVEs monthly across all images, prioritizing by CVSS and internet exposure","Move to minimal or distroless bases via multi-stage builds, with centrally automated rebuilds","Enforce non-root users and read-only root filesystems for all workloads via admission","Deploy a runtime security agent that detects shell spawns and unexpected processes inside containers"],"a":[1],"e":"Most of the CVEs come from packages the app doesn't need. Minimal bases with multi-stage builds remove them and shrink the attack surface, and central rebuilds keep it current. Monthly patching treats the symptoms of a bloated base. Non-root and read-only are valuable hardening but don't reduce the vulnerable packages. Runtime detection finds exploitation after it starts."},
{"id":"p51","d":5,"q":"An internet-facing claims app has a critical authorization flaw. The fix needs a data model change, estimated at three months. The product owner emailed security saying 'I accept the risk' and added a backlog item. Company policy says exceptions for critical internet-facing findings need sign-off from the business unit VP and the CISO. A WAF rule could block the specific request pattern used in the pen test. What is the MOST important next step?","o":["Record the product owner's email as the acceptance and track the backlog item through to the fix","Have the security team accept the risk itself, since it understands the technical exposure best","Take the app offline until the data model change is complete and verified by a retest","Raise a formal exception with VP and CISO sign-off, the WAF control, and a dated fix plan"],"a":[3],"e":"Risk must be accepted by someone with the authority the policy requires, with compensating controls and an expiry tied to the fix. The product owner lacks that authority for a critical internet-facing finding. Security advises but shouldn't own business risk. Taking the app offline may be disproportionate when a compensating control exists, and that is the business's call to make."},
{"id":"p52","d":5,"q":"A bank's online statements page is legitimately embedded in iframes by two partner portals, partner-a.com and partner-b.com. A pen test showed that any other site can also frame it, enabling clickjacking on the 'download statement' and 'update email' buttons. The app currently sends no framing headers. Users are on current browsers. Which control is MOST appropriate?","o":["Send X-Frame-Options: DENY on all responses to block framing of the statements page","Send Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors listing 'self' and the two partner origins","Send X-Frame-Options: ALLOW-FROM for the two partner origins on the statements page","Add frame-busting JavaScript that redirects the top window when the page is not top-level"],"a":[1],"e":"CSP frame-ancestors supports a list of allowed origins and is honored by current browsers, so it blocks other sites while keeping the partners working. DENY would break the partners. ALLOW-FROM is obsolete and ignored by modern browsers, and it only supported one origin anyway. Frame-busting scripts can be defeated by sandboxed iframes."},
{"id":"p53","d":5,"q":"An agile team building a patient scheduling app works in two-week sprints. The company's application security standard is a 60-page document, and developers say they don't read it. Security issues keep turning up late in the cycle. The team has a security champion who attends refinement. The product owner is worried about velocity. Which approach BEST builds security into the team's work?","o":["Attach the full standard to each epic and have the champion confirm compliance at review","Run a quick threat model on risky stories in refinement and turn the results into acceptance criteria","Schedule a dedicated security hardening sprint each quarter to work down issues found late in the cycle","Have the central security team keep a separate security backlog that the team pulls from when it has capacity"],"a":[1],"e":"Turning a short threat model of each risky story into concrete acceptance criteria makes security part of normal 'done' and keeps it small enough to fit the sprint. Attaching the full standard repeats what already fails. Hardening sprints formalize finding issues late. A separate backlog makes security optional work that loses to features."},
{"id":"p54","d":5,"q":"A health app shows lab results and stores downloaded PDFs on the device. A privacy review found copies of the PDFs in users' cloud device backups, which are outside the company's control. The team has already added certificate pinning and disabled screenshots on the results screen. The app runs on iOS and Android. Which control BEST addresses the finding?","o":["Hide app content in the task switcher snapshot so results aren't visible when the app is in the background","Exclude the PDFs from device backups and keep them in protected storage tied to the device's lock state","Disable copy and paste on screens that display lab values so users can't move data into other apps","Encrypt the PDFs with a key stored in shared preferences so backup copies are harder to read"],"a":[1],"e":"The finding is about backups, so excluding the files from backup and using platform data protection addresses it directly. Hiding snapshots and blocking the clipboard deal with other leakage paths. A key in shared preferences is likely backed up with the files, so the encryption adds little protection."},
{"id":"p55","d":5,"q":"A payments company is launching a public API in eight weeks. Thousands of third-party developers will build apps that act on behalf of the company's merchants, such as reading transactions and issuing refunds. The security team already runs a gateway with WAF rules. Some large partners have asked to use mTLS. Which authentication and authorization model fits BEST as the foundation?","o":["Static API keys issued per developer, with keys rotated each year and usage tracked per key at the gateway","OAuth 2.0 authorization code with PKCE, granular scopes, merchant consent, and per-client quotas","mTLS client certificates for all developers, issued by the company's CA and checked at the gateway","IP allowlisting of each developer's servers combined with HTTP basic authentication over TLS"],"a":[1],"e":"Third-party apps acting for merchants need delegated, consented, scoped access, which is what OAuth authorization code with PKCE provides, along with quotas for abuse control. API keys identify the developer but not which merchant agreed to what. mTLS can be offered to large partners as an extra binding but is heavy as the base for thousands of developers. IP lists and basic auth don't scale and give no delegation."},
{"id":"p56","d":5,"q":"A web app's error pages show full stack traces, database names, and framework versions. The operations team says they rely on these pages to troubleshoot, because they lack access to the application logs. The app sits behind a WAF that can rewrite responses. An external audit is scheduled for next month, and the app's framework is two major versions behind. What is the BEST fix?","o":["Return a generic error with a correlation ID and log full details server-side, with ops log access","Add a WAF response rule that strips stack traces and version strings from error pages before they leave","Show detailed errors only to the corporate IP range and generic ones to everyone else","Turn off error logging in production so sensitive details aren't recorded anywhere outside the database"],"a":[0],"e":"Generic messages with a correlation ID stop information leakage, and logging details server-side with proper ops access meets the troubleshooting need. WAF rewriting is fragile and misses new formats. IP-based display trusts network location and can leak through proxies. Turning off error logging hurts operations and incident response."},
{"id":"p57","d":5,"q":"A retailer wants a 30-day 'remember me' option on its website. Requirements: users must be logged out of all devices when they change their password, and sensitive actions like changing the shipping address must still ask for the password. The architecture team prefers stateless services, and the session store is Redis. Which design BEST meets the requirements?","o":["Extend the session cookie lifetime to 30 days and rotate the session ID each time the user signs in","Issue a stateless JWT with a 30-day expiry in a secure cookie, validated by signature on each request","Issue a separate random token, stored hashed and rotated on use, with step-up auth for sensitive actions","Store the username and an encrypted password in a persistent cookie so the app can sign the user back in"],"a":[2],"e":"A separate server-tracked token can be revoked on password change and rotated to detect theft, and step-up for sensitive actions limits its power. A 30-day session cookie gives full session power for a month. A stateless JWT can't be revoked without adding the server state the team wanted to avoid. Storing credentials in a cookie exposes them to theft."},
{"id":"p58","d":5,"q":"A new reporting tool needs to show monthly sales totals by region. The database also holds customer names, card tokens, and loyalty details. The DBA proposes granting the tool's account the existing read-only role on the whole sales schema, since 'it can't change anything.' The reporting vendor connects from its own SaaS platform. What is the BEST design?","o":["Grant the existing read-only role on the sales schema, since the account can't modify the data","Point the tool at a read replica with read access to the full schema to protect production performance","Create a dedicated account with SELECT only on views that expose the aggregated regional totals","Encrypt the customer and loyalty columns and withhold the keys from the reporting account"],"a":[2],"e":"Least privilege here means access only to the aggregated data the tool needs, through purpose-built views. A read-only schema role still exposes personal data to a third-party platform. A replica helps performance but has the same exposure. Column encryption protects some fields but still lets the account read everything else and depends on catching each sensitive column."},
{"id":"p59","d":5,"q":"A legacy web app uses cookie-based sessions with no explicit SameSite attribute, so modern browsers treat the cookie as Lax. Several state-changing actions are implemented as GET requests, such as /account/close?confirm=1 and /email/change?to=. The team believes default Lax handling means CSRF is no longer a concern. Some users arrive through emailed deep links. What should the architect require?","o":["Rely on the browser's default Lax behavior, and document the decision as an accepted design choice","Make state-changing actions POST-only and require anti-CSRF tokens validated on the server","Validate the Referer header on state-changing requests and reject those from other sites","Set SameSite=Strict on the session cookie so it's not sent on cross-site navigation"],"a":[1],"e":"Lax still sends cookies on top-level GET navigation, so state-changing GETs remain exploitable. Moving them to POST and adding server-checked tokens is the durable fix. Referer checks break when the header is stripped and are easy to get wrong. Strict would block the attack but also breaks the emailed deep links that users rely on, and leaves unsafe GET design in place."},
{"id":"p60","d":5,"q":"A company runs about 120 services: most on Kubernetes, about 30 on VMs. Each team requests TLS certificates manually and tracks expiry in a spreadsheet. Two outages last year came from expired certificates, and the security team wants service-to-service mTLS everywhere. A service mesh pilot is running in one Kubernetes cluster. Which improvement is MOST appropriate?","o":["Issue two-year certificates from the internal CA so teams renew less often and outages become rare","Add certificate monitoring that alerts teams 30 days before expiry","Adopt a workload identity platform issuing short-lived certificates to Kubernetes and VM workloads","Roll out the service mesh to all Kubernetes clusters, which automates certificates for the services there"],"a":[2],"e":"Automated, short-lived workload certificates remove the manual renewals that caused outages and support mTLS across both Kubernetes and VMs. Longer certificates reduce renewals but extend exposure and keep the manual process. Monitoring is a useful detective control but leaves the toil. A Kubernetes-only mesh leaves the 30 VM services out."},
{"id":"p61","d":5,"q":"A checkout flow passes item prices in hidden form fields across three steps, and the final step charges whatever price comes back from the browser. A tester bought a laptop for one dollar. The team proposes signing the price with an HMAC so tampering is detected. Prices change several times a day during promotions, and coupons are single-use. What should the architect require?","o":["Sign each price with an HMAC as proposed and verify the signature at the final step before charging","Keep the cart server-side and recompute prices and discounts from the catalog at charge time","Encrypt the hidden price fields with AES so users can't read or edit them","Validate that the submitted price falls within an expected range for that product before charging"],"a":[1],"e":"The server is the only trustworthy source of price, so recomputing from server-side state at charge time fixes it. HMAC signing detects edits but allows replay of old signed prices after a promotion ends or a coupon is used. Encryption without integrity can still be tampered with or replayed. Range checks allow abuse within the range."},
{"id":"p62","d":5,"q":"A company wants to ensure that only approved executables and scripts run on its production Windows and Linux servers. EDR is deployed everywhere, servers are built from golden images, and file integrity monitoring alerts on changes to system directories. A red team still ran its own tools from a temp directory without being stopped. Which control BEST closes this gap at the host level?","o":["Tune EDR rules to alert on unsigned binaries run from temp directories","Application allowlisting through code integrity policies, such as WDAC and fapolicyd","Rebuild servers from golden images weekly so dropped tools are removed on a regular schedule","Extend file integrity monitoring to temp directories and alert on new executable files there"],"a":[1],"e":"Allowlisting enforces which code may run, so unapproved tools are blocked rather than reported. EDR and FIM are detective and the red team ran under them. Weekly rebuilds clear tools after the fact but don't stop them running in between."},
{"id":"p63","d":5,"q":"An architect is defining input validation for a public REST API. Most fields are structured: IDs, dates, amounts, country codes. A few are free text, like delivery notes, which must accept punctuation and multiple languages. The data ends up in a SQL database, HTML emails, and a PDF generator. A WAF is in front of the API. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Reject requests with denylisted characters like quotes, angle brackets, and semicolons","Strip HTML tags and SQL keywords from all string fields at input so stored data is safe for later use","Validate against a strict schema, and handle free text with context-specific encoding at each sink","Rely on WAF rules tuned for the API's endpoints, since they inspect the requests before they reach the API"],"a":[2],"e":"Strict schema validation suits structured fields, while free text can't be allowlisted by character and needs safe handling at each sink: parameterized SQL, HTML encoding in emails, and safe PDF generation. Denylists break legitimate text and miss encodings. Stripping at input corrupts data and doesn't match each output context. A WAF is a backstop, not the validation design."},
{"id":"p64","d":5,"q":"A marketing platform builds email bodies by concatenating the customer's display name into a Jinja template string and then rendering it. A tester set their display name to {{7*7}} and the email showed 49. The platform sends two million emails a month, and the team is mid-migration to a new email provider. Autoescaping is enabled. What should the architect require?","o":["HTML-encode the display name before concatenating it into the template so it's treated as text","Render a fixed template and pass the display name in only as a context variable","Switch to a sandboxed Jinja environment so injected expressions can't reach dangerous objects","Strip braces and percent signs from display names on save"],"a":[1],"e":"This is server-side template injection because user input becomes template code. Keeping templates fixed and passing user data only as variables removes the injection. HTML encoding doesn't touch braces, so expressions still run. A sandbox reduces impact but keeps the flawed pattern and has a history of escapes. Stripping characters is a denylist that alters legitimate names and misses other syntax."},
{"id":"p65","d":5,"q":"A CFO asks whether the company's two-million-dollar application security investment over two years is reducing risk. The AppSec team currently reports monthly counts of scanner findings, pen tests completed, and developers trained. Finding counts went up after new tools were added. The board meets next quarter. Which metric BEST answers the CFO's question?","o":["Total findings identified per month across SAST, DAST, and SCA, with a breakdown by business unit","Percentage of developers who completed secure coding training, tracked against the annual target","Trend in high-severity findings open past SLA and the mean time to remediate them","Pen tests completed each quarter versus the number of apps in scope"],"a":[2],"e":"Risk reduction shows up in how fast serious issues get fixed and how many stay open past SLA. Finding counts rise with better tooling, so they measure detection, not risk. Training completion and pen test counts measure activity, not outcomes."}
);
